    {
      "id": "sticky_overview",
      "name": "Overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -16,
        -480
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 1,
        "width": 2240,
        "height": 380,
        "content": "## Email Security Triage Agent\n\nWatches your inbox and sorts every new email into one of five categories — Possible Fraud, Urgent, Ads, Review, or Not Important — then applies the matching Gmail label automatically.\n\n### How it works\nEvery minute it checks for new unread mail. For each email, an AI agent reads the subject, sender, and preview text and decides which category fits, leaning toward \"Review\" rather than guessing when something looks fraud-like. It applies the matching label straight in Gmail — nothing is moved, archived, or deleted, so your inbox looks the same, just labeled. Every decision is also logged to a Google Sheet (subject, sender, category, time) so you have a running record of what came in and how it was sorted.\n\n### Setup\n1. In Gmail, create 5 labels with these exact names: ⚠️ Possible Fraud, 🔴 Urgent, 📢 Ads, 🔎 Review, ⚪ Not Important.\n2. Open \"Set log sheet details\" and enter your Google Sheet ID and tab name (header row: subject, from, category, classified_at).\n3. Connect your Gmail, Google Sheets, and Google Gemini credentials.\n4. Turn the workflow on.\n\n### Customization tips\nTighten or loosen the fraud wording in the AI agent's system prompt, or swap the Google Sheet log for n8n's Data Table."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky_note_a",
      "name": "Note: reading the email",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -16,
        -80
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 2,
        "width": 880,
        "height": 190,
        "content": "## Reading the email\nPicks up each new unread email, then looks up the account's current Gmail labels so every category name can be matched to its real label ID — done fresh each run, since label IDs are unique to every Gmail account."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky_note_b",
      "name": "Note: deciding the category",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        880,
        -80
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 2,
        "width": 820,
        "height": 190,
        "content": "## Deciding the category\nAn AI agent reads each email's subject, sender, and preview, then picks one of the five categories — favoring caution over guessing when something looks fraud-like — and applies the matching Gmail label itself."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "sticky_note_c",
      "name": "Note: recording the result",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1728,
